Open a phone's new-tab links in the tab the user is already on.

Tabs live behind a grid on a phone, so a link with target=_blank opens one the
user never sees, and back does not return to where they came from because the
new tab has no history. Navigating in place keeps both.

Only link navigations are affected, and deliberately so. A target=_blank link
comes through OpenURLFromTab, and since Chrome 88 such a link implies noopener
-- nothing on the page holds a handle to the window that would have appeared,
so nothing breaks by not creating it. window.open() takes another path
entirely: it needs a browsing context and arrives through AddNewContents, so
an OAuth or payment popup that reads the window it opened is untouched. Where
it cannot be known, the new tab stays, which is what was asked for.

Renderer-initiated only, so the shell's "open in new tab", the newTab command
and restoring a closed tab all still open one.

Phones only, read from ui_family at the moment of the click, so unfolding a
foldable changes the behaviour with it and no preference is involved.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_web_contents_delegate/browser_web_contents_delegate.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_web_contents_delegate/browser_web_contents_delegate.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_web_contents_delegate/browser_web_contents_delegate.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_web_contents_delegate/browser_web_contents_delegate.cc
@@ -571,6 +571,34 @@
   DCHECK(params.Valid());
 #endif
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // On a phone, a link that asks for a new tab gets the current one instead.
+  // Tabs live behind a grid there, so the new one arrives unannounced, and
+  // back does not return to the page the user came from because the new tab
+  // has no history. Navigating in place keeps both.
+  //
+  // Deliberately only here. A link with target=_blank is routed through this
+  // function, and since Chrome 88 such a link implies noopener, so nothing on
+  // the page is holding a handle to the window that would have appeared.
+  // window.open() takes another path entirely -- it needs a browsing context
+  // and goes through AddNewContents -- so a login or payment popup that reads
+  // the window it opened is untouched. That was the instruction: where it
+  // cannot be known, keep the new tab.
+  //
+  // Renderer-initiated only, so the shell's own "open in new tab", the newTab
+  // command and restoring a closed tab all still open one.
+  content::OpenURLParams phone_params = params;
+  if (chrome::ohos::IsAuraShellMobilePhoneUi() && params.is_renderer_initiated &&
+      (params.disposition == WindowOpenDisposition::NEW_FOREGROUND_TAB ||
+       params.disposition == WindowOpenDisposition::NEW_BACKGROUND_TAB)) {
+    phone_params.disposition = WindowOpenDisposition::CURRENT_TAB;
+    // Straight back in with the disposition changed. The guard above is false
+    // the second time, so this goes once and no further.
+    return OpenURLFromTab(source, phone_params,
+                          std::move(navigation_handle_callback));
+  }
+#endif
+
   if ((browser_->GetType() == BrowserWindowInterface::Type::TYPE_DEVTOOLS)) {
     DevToolsWindow* window = DevToolsWindow::AsDevToolsWindow(source);
     DCHECK(window);
